{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's the night of the 9th July, 1962. On a small \natoll in the Marshall islands the sky is clear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7400,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "except for a thick band of clouds on the horizon. \nSuddenly the eastern horizon lights up--but it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not sunrise. A white flash burns through the dense \nclouds, and from that flash grows a vast sphere  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of green light. Nebulous white filaments leap \nfrom the ball and arch across the sky,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before fading as a chain of circular shockwaves \nerupted from the site of the flash. It's over in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33600,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less than a minute. But then a dull burning \nred glow grows across the eastern sky,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cut with enormous white rainbows, and lasts \nfor an hour and a half. If there was anyone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the arctic present on that near-equatorial \nisland they may have recognized the tell-tail  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns of aurora dancing in the sky. \nBut this was no natural aurora. It was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the result of a thermonuclear detonation \nin outer space--400km above another small  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63880,
    "dDurationMs": 9920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atoll 1400 km distant to the east. This was the Starfish \nPrime test, and its unexpected devastation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73800,
    "dDurationMs": 7786,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "helped usher in an era of nuclear peace. \nAn era that may, or may not, persist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Starfish Prime was the largest of the \nfive thermonuclear tests that the United  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "States conducted in space. There was a big \nbang, and there was radioactive fallout,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96720,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is its own troublesome tale. But today we’re \ntelling the story of the 10^29 electrons that were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104360,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produced in the explosion or released as gamma \nrays ionized the upper atmosphere. These particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spiralled along the Earth’s magnetic field lines, \nleading to the artificial auroras I described,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116880,
    "dDurationMs": 8200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also leading to the creation of a new radiation belt several orders  of magnitude stronger than the Earth’s natural  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125080,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "belts and they lingered for 5 years. A third of all low Earth satellites were destroyed due to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133640,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "repeated passage through this belt. Admittedly \nat that time a third of LEO satellites was six  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140720,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "satellites. Today that would be thousands. \nAnd right after the explosion, a cascade of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147520,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ionization caused an electromagnetic pulse—an \nEMP—that disrupted electronics over much of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154520,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pacific ocean. In Honolulu, 900km from the blast, \nhundreds of streetlights just went dark."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162080,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Starfish Prime was a devastatingly clear \ndemonstration of the destructive power of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167320,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclear weapons in space. Clear enough that in \n1963 the world’s main nuclear powers ratified  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174240,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Partial Nuclear Test Ban Treaty banning \nspace and under-ocean nuclear testing. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181080,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was followed in 1967 with Outer Space Treaty, a \nbroad agreement for the peaceful use of space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188600,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and clearly bans any nuclear or other weapons of \nmass destruction being used or staged in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195200,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But treaties are only good as far \nas we obey them. In early February,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there were media reports that US intelligence \nhad evidence that Russia is developing a new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205560,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space-based military nuclear capability. There \nwas frustratingly little detail—we don’t even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know what type of facility this might be. But \nvarious pundits have speculated that it could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216960,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be about developing the capacity to perform EMP \nattacks—either an actual EMP-generating satellite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the capability for detonating nuclear \nexplosions in orbit, similar to Starfish."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This episode isn’t about what Russia or any other \nnation may or may not be doing. But in light of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the media rumour mill, it seems like a good time \nto bring science to one aspect of this issue.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241560,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What happens if we nuke space? What would happen \nif a Starfish Prime-like event happened today?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249160,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with what’s probably the best known \neffect of nuking space—the electromagnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253600,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pulse—the EMP, because that’s the main direct \neffect on the ground. EMPs are all over science  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fiction—great for when you want to shut down a \nspaceship without destroying it. They’re also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a handy way to obliterate modern technology \nto kickstart your post-apocalyptic drama. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270800,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "EMPs aren’t science fiction. Real militaries \nare experimenting on real EMP generators,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and as Starfish Prime showed us, \nspace nukes can send powerful EMPs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the surface. So what exactly is \nan EMP, and how dangerous are they?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287160,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with the EM part of the EMP. An EM \nwave—an electromagnetic wave is—a self-propagating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ripple of electric and magnetic field caused when \ncharged particles accelerate. They travel at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed of light—because light is an EM wave! When \nsuch a wave encounters another charged particle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305080,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those rippling electric and magnetic \nfields cause that particle to jiggle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309280,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effectively transferring energy and \ninformation between two locations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313960,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The wavelength of the wave determines the scale \nof the jiggling. Microscopic wavelengths jiggle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319120,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things on the scale of atoms, for example \nelectrons jumping between atomic energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323120,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "levels on your computer screen create EM waves \nseveral hundred nanometers in wavelength—just the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329200,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right wavelength needed jiggle electrons in your \nretina—this is visible light. A current bouncing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up and down a distant antenna creates an EM wave \nfrom centimeters to meters in wavelength—radio  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342320,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves—and these cause electrons in your \ncell phone’s antenna to jiggle in unison."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348480,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what about an EM pulse? These are very short, \nvery intense bursts of radio-wavelength EM waves.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357040,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They cause electrons to jiggle in wires, just \nlike any radio signal. The difference here is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the intensity is so strong that \nit causes a lot of electrons to move,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "potentially sending high currents through wires or \neven circuitry. Generating excess current in, say,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computer circuitry will at best cause the computer \nto shut down. But current also causes heat,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and heat melts wire. A large EM pulse will \nfry electronics. Generating too much excess  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current in a national power grid will melt \ntransformer coils and shut down the grid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391240,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To make an artificial EMP you basically \nneed to apply an extremely high voltage  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396240,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across a conducting coil. The energy requirement \ntypically means you can only do this for a very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401240,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "short time. The simplest EMP device consists \nof a bank of capacitors connected to a power  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408280,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "source and multiple loops of wire, with the circuit broken \nby a spark gap. Charge will build up in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capacitors until there’s sufficient voltage \nfor the air between the charge gap to ionize  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and close the circuit. All those stored \nelectrons suddenly flow through the loop,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424960,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generating an enormous magnetic \nfield which kick-starts the EMP."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430200,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That seems like you need a pretty specific \nset of conditions to generate an EMP. And so it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436480,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems surprising that a giant explosion \nin the sky produces an EMP by basically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441400,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same mechanism. Where does the electrical \ncurrent come from? Why is it moving in circles?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447520,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And how does the resulting electromagnetic \nradiation pile up into such a sharp pulse?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454080,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start by remembering how nukes \nwork. In general we’re talking about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458520,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modern thermonuclear bombs here. These work \nby compressing a bunch of hydrogen to such  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463440,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high density that it mimics the processes \nin the heart of a star—hydrogen fuses to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469240,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "helium and releases an enormous \namount of energy in the process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472480,
    "dDurationMs": 440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "…"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472920,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A small fraction of this energy is released \nin the form of a very rapid burst of gamma  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rays—the highest energy electromagnetic radiation. \nNone of that radiation makes it to the ground,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thankfully. To gamma rays and also X-rays, Earth’s \natmosphere is basically opaque—they can travel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very little distance before bumping into some \nair molecule. But although the actual gamma ray  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons don’t make it to the ground, some of their \nenergy does. When a gamma ray hits a molecule,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501680,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it typically scatters off an electron. \nThe energy of gamma rays is so extreme  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the electron is knocked free of its \natom and sent hurtling downwards in roughly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512040,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same direction as the gamma ray, which \nitself continues on but with reduced energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516640,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that fast-moving electron finds itself in \nthe presence of a magnetic field—the Earth’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geomagnetic field. Magnetic fields \nexert a force on moving charges,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and this charge is moving fast. The electron \nbegins to spiral around the geomagnetic field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533000,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lines. Remember how we made our artificial EMP? \nBy sending a huge current around a loop? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540000,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here’s a current moving in a loop. The spiraling \nelectron creates a miniscule EMP that travels in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same direction as the original gamma ray. This \ndoesn’t sound too dangerous yet. But remember that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552400,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the EMP travels at the speed of light—the same \nspeed as the gamma ray that created it. It keeps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up with that photon, and so further scattering \nevents will add to the strength of this one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563240,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "narrow pulse. In fact, as the entire thin shell \nof gamma rays passes through the atmosphere,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569440,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it gets converted one scattering event at \na time into the same increasingly powerful  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574600,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetic pulse that always keeps \npace with the fading gamma ray shell. By  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580000,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the time the shell is gone, the pulse \nis powerful enough to do its damage."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585240,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The potency and range of such an EMP depends on \nthe size of the explosion and the altitude.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591520,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, any explosion from orbit \nwill affect a continent-scale region,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596680,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultimately limited by the curvature of the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598920,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The 400km-high Starfish prime explosion blew out transformers 1400km away in Hawaii.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606280,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s estimated that a similar thermonuclear \ndetonation above a large, populared landmass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would cause massive currents in the electrical \ncables that now criss-cross the continent—burning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out hundreds of the insufficiently protected \ntransformers connecting this network. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would result in blackouts across that continent and could take years to repair. The effect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be similar to us getting a direct hit \nfrom a major coronal mass ejection from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun—a blast from a solar magnetic storm like the one that smacked us back in 1859, before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we had an electrical grid to worry about, or that \nblacked out all Quebec in 1989. Probably we should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646080,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "put more of the relatively cheap safeguards \nin place to avoid the multi-trillion dollar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651320,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cost of repair after an electromagnetic \nincident—whether natural or otherwise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657680,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So space nukes can make a mess on \nthe ground. They’re also not very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662040,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good for space. I told you that the \nStarfish Prime test wiped out low  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667120,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earth orbit satellites. Let’s \nsee exactly how that happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671960,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The EMP we just talked about is generated \nby electrons spiraling along the Earth’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676640,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic field lines. Those electrons \ndon’t go away after generating the pulse.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681720,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As they get closer to the ground near the poles, the changing angle of the field lines means  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687920,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pitch of their loops relative to those \nfield lines change. When the pitch is inverted,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692680,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the magnetic force on the electrons pushes them \nin the opposite direction. They’re reflected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697160,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before they reach the ground and start traveling back up the field. This happens again when they reach  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702640,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the opposite end of the field line on the opposite \nhemisphere. In this way, an electron can bounce  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707880,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back and forth until getting absorbed by the \natmosphere or knocked free by a solar photon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But until most of the electrons are so \nliberated, they remain bound in this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718080,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "band that traces the geomagnetic field. At the \nsame time, east-west smearing of the electrons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causes the band to form a toroidal belt \naround the whole earth. A radiation belt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731240,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth has two natural radiation belts—the Van Alen \nbelts—discovered in 1958, only a few years before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737920,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Starfish Prime test. These are also made of \ncharged particles bouncing along our geomagnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field lines—but here they’re mostly high-energy \nelectrons and protons from the solar wind,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with some cosmic rays for good measure. They \nextend from around a couple of thousand to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753400,
    "dDurationMs": 8680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "60000km above the Earth, with a gap between \nthem at around 2-4 Earth radii. We do not put  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762080,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "satellites in the Van Allen belts because their \nelectronics would degrade pretty quickly. We’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768960,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lucky that geosynchronous orbits are in this \nsafe zone between the belts. Low-earth orbit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774680,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "satellites are also safe if they stay below the \ninner belt—below around 2000km. And these days,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781520,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that part of space is pretty densely \npacked with something like 5,000 satellites."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787920,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you can imagine the problem if a Starfish \nPrime-like event happened today. A space nuke  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794800,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can target a specific if very large region on the ground with a EMP, but it’s indiscriminate in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its damage to satellites. Low Earth Orbit would \ngain a charged particle density similar to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "peak of the Van Allen Belts, perhaps wiping out a \ngood fraction of satellites there. Hopefully this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a strong enough deterrent against such \nan attack by a nation dependent on those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819840,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "satellites—which includes most nations \ncapable of launching such an attack."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824280,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are ways to harden satellites \nagainst increased charge particle flux  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828640,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a new Starfish Prime-like \nevent. But that’s expensive.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832600,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The United States is moving more \nin the direction of redundancy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "having just launched a prototype to test its \nProliferated Warfighter Space Architecture.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This program aims to blanket low-Earth orbit with \nhundreds of small Starlink-like satellites. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848120,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "philosophy is that it’s harder to wipe out \nmany small, cheap satellites—whether with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852400,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an artificial radiation belt or a targeted \nEMP, and they’re also easier to replace."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859320,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The effects of the Starfish Prime test were \nfar more widespread and damaging than anyone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864560,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expected. But the silver lining to this mushroom \ncloud is that the world came together to first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871920,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ban the most irresponsible nuclear tests, and \nthen to commit to the peaceful use of space with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Outer Space Treaty. But good intentions don’t \nlast forever. Given the global existential threat  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of putting nukes in space, we should choose \ncarefully. Do we want to move into an era of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889800,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "militarized space? Or do we reaffirm our commitment to our 60 year-long peaceful space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
